<speak>
Chapter One. The Kid in Room Four.<break time="1200ms"/>
Kade Mercer had been at Northline Alternative for forty-three school days, which was long enough to know that Room Four had three reliable clocks<break time="250ms"/> and none of them told time.<break time="800ms"/>
The first was the white plastic clock above the door. Its second hand twitched twice for every second, and stopped whenever the heater kicked on.<break time="700ms"/>
The second was Ms. Alvarez's coffee.<break time="400ms"/> Full meant first period.<break time="300ms"/> Half meant lunch was getting close.<break time="300ms"/> Empty meant nobody should ask her anything<break time="200ms"/> unless someone was bleeding.<break time="800ms"/>
The third was Darius Bell.<break time="800ms"/>
At exactly ten seventeen every morning, Darius decided he had endured enough education for one day, stood up, announced a destination that was obviously false, and walked out.<break time="600ms"/>
Today he made it to ten nineteen.<break time="800ms"/>
"Bathroom," Darius said.<break time="500ms"/>
Ms. Alvarez did not look up from the district tablet in her hands.<break time="450ms"/>
"You went fourteen minutes ago."<break time="400ms"/>
"Different bathroom."<break time="400ms"/>
"There is one bathroom."<break time="500ms"/>
Darius considered that.<break time="450ms"/>
"Then I forgot something in it."<break time="800ms"/>
||
Across the room, Kade kept his eyes on his own tablet and moved his thumb over the blank answer field without touching it.<break time="600ms"/>
The question read:<break time="600ms"/>
<prosody rate="90%">A cargo tram with a mass of eighteen thousand kilograms accelerates from rest to twelve meters per second over eight seconds. What average force is required?</prosody><break time="800ms"/>
It was a stupid question.<break time="600ms"/>
Not mathematically. Mathematically it was almost insulting.<break time="600ms"/>
The problem was the word<break time="200ms"/> required.<break time="700ms"/>
Required by what?<break time="700ms"/>
If the tram was on rails, friction mattered. If it was magnetic, the field geometry mattered. If twelve meters per second was measured relative to the track, fine<break time="200ms"/> but if the track itself was moving<break time="500ms"/>
Kade exhaled through his nose and typed the answer they wanted.<break time="600ms"/>
<prosody rate="90%">Twenty-seven thousand newtons.</prosody><break time="600ms"/>
Green check.<break time="500ms"/>
A cartoon rocket ship appeared and performed a tiny victory loop.<break time="600ms"/>
Kade hated the rocket ship.<break time="800ms"/>
"Mercer."<break time="500ms"/>
He looked up.<break time="450ms"/>
Ms. Alvarez pointed at Darius without moving anything but her eyes.<break time="500ms"/>
Darius had reached the door.<break time="500ms"/>
Kade looked at him.<break time="450ms"/>
"You know she has to log it if you leave again."<break time="500ms"/>
Darius's hand stopped on the push bar.<break time="450ms"/>
"Don't start."<break time="350ms"/>
"I didn't."<break time="350ms"/>
"You got that face."<break time="350ms"/>
"What face?"<break time="400ms"/>
"The one where you think you're smarter than everybody."<break time="500ms"/>
Kade leaned back in his chair.<break time="450ms"/>
"I don't have enough facial muscles for that."<break time="650ms"/>
A laugh escaped from somewhere behind him.<break time="500ms"/>
Darius did not smile.<break time="700ms"/>
||
He was sixteen, two inches taller than Kade, and built like the district had accidentally enrolled a linebacker in remedial geometry. He had been suspended from Canyon Ridge High after putting another kid through the glass panel beside a classroom door. The other kid needed eleven stitches.<break time="600ms"/>
Darius needed none.<break time="700ms"/>
Northline had taught Kade that none of those facts meant what people assumed they meant.<break time="800ms"/>
"I'm leaving," Darius said.<break time="400ms"/>
"Okay."<break time="350ms"/>
"You gonna stop me?"<break time="350ms"/>
"No."<break time="550ms"/>
That irritated him more.<break time="600ms"/>
Kade turned his tablet around just enough for Darius to see the red banner in the upper corner.<break time="600ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Attendance contract.<break time="250ms"/> Four of five unexcused exits.</prosody><break time="800ms"/>
Darius stared at it.<break time="500ms"/>
"One more," Kade said, "and Russell sends you back to review."<break time="400ms"/>
"So?"<break time="350ms"/>
"So your review is Friday."<break time="600ms"/>
Darius's jaw tightened.<break time="600ms"/>
Kade knew about Friday because Darius had spent most of the previous afternoon pretending not to care whether his mother could get off work for the meeting.<break time="700ms"/>
"If they push it another week," Kade said, "your mom misses another shift for nothing."<break time="600ms"/>
Darius looked toward Ms. Alvarez.<break time="500ms"/>
She was studying her tablet with the intense concentration of someone who had absolutely not heard the conversation.<break time="700ms"/>
"Sit down," Kade said. "Walk out at lunch and call it rebellion."<break time="650ms"/>
Darius stared at him for another two seconds.<break time="500ms"/>
Then he came back to his chair.<break time="600ms"/>
"Still hate you," he muttered.<break time="400ms"/>
"I know."<break time="700ms"/>
Ms. Alvarez lifted her coffee and drank.<break time="500ms"/>
Still half full.<break time="400ms"/>
Not lunch yet.<break time="1200ms"/>
||
Northline Alternative occupied an old county records building between a tire warehouse and a self-storage facility. Someone had painted the exterior blue three years earlier, but the desert sun had bleached the south wall to a color best described as disappointed gray.<break time="700ms"/>
The district website called it a personalized transitional learning campus.<break time="500ms"/>
Students called it Northline.<break time="500ms"/>
Parents called it whatever they needed to call it when relatives asked where their kid went to school now.<break time="800ms"/>
There were forty-two students enrolled, divided among six classrooms. Some had been expelled. Some had been arrested. Some had stopped showing up at regular school until the district stopped pretending they would. A few had panic disorders, complicated families, pregnancies, probation officers, jobs, addictions, or combinations of all six.<break time="700ms"/>
Northline did not have a mascot.<break time="600ms"/>
It had a metal detector, two social workers, one security officer who spent most afternoons fixing bicycles, and a vending machine that occasionally dispensed three bags of pretzels for the price of one.<break time="600ms"/>
Nobody knew why.<break time="500ms"/>
Darius believed the machine respected him.<break time="900ms"/>
Kade had none of the obvious reasons for being there.<break time="600ms"/>
That was, according to Vice Principal Russell, part of his problem.<break time="800ms"/>
At seventeen, Kade had collected three suspensions, two school transfers, a semester of failed classes he could have passed, and enough teacher comments to assemble a personality entirely from administrative adjectives.<break time="800ms"/>
<prosody rate="90%">Bright but inconsistent.<break time="450ms"/> Argumentative.<break time="450ms"/> Fails to demonstrate appropriate investment.<break time="450ms"/> Challenges authority.<break time="450ms"/> Does not utilize available opportunities.</prosody><break time="900ms"/>
His favorite came from ninth grade.<break time="600ms"/>
<prosody rate="90%">Kade often appears disengaged even when he understands the material.</prosody><break time="800ms"/>
As if understanding it should have made pretending to enjoy it easier.<break time="800ms"/>
He had read the whole file once while Russell was out of the office.<break time="600ms"/>
Not hacked it.<break time="500ms"/>
That was an important distinction.<break time="700ms"/>
Russell had left the file open on his desk while he went to make a phone call, which in Kade's opinion transformed unauthorized access into poor information security.<break time="700ms"/>
Ms. Alvarez disagreed.<break time="500ms"/>
They had argued about that for eleven minutes.<break time="500ms"/>
Kade had enjoyed the argument.<break time="450ms"/>
She had not.<break time="500ms"/>
Probably.<break time="1200ms"/>
At ten thirty-one, the classroom speaker clicked.<break time="600ms"/>
Every head lifted.<break time="500ms"/>
Northline's speaker system usually worked only when nobody wanted it to.<break time="700ms"/>
<prosody rate="92%">"Attention, students. All seventeen-year-old students scheduled for the National Aptitude Ascension Examination should report to Multipurpose Two at eleven hundred hours. Bring your district identification card. Testing begins promptly at eleven fifteen."</prosody><break time="800ms"/>
Darius groaned.<break time="450ms"/>
"You're sixteen," Kade said.<break time="350ms"/>
"I'm groaning in solidarity."<break time="350ms"/>
"That's new."<break time="350ms"/>
"I'm growing."<break time="650ms"/>
The speaker clicked off.<break time="600ms"/>
Ms. Alvarez set down her tablet.<break time="500ms"/>
"Mercer."<break time="450ms"/>
Kade looked at her.<break time="450ms"/>
"You have your ID?"<break time="350ms"/>
"Somewhere."<break time="350ms"/>
"Find somewhere."<break time="500ms"/>
He patted the pocket of his hoodie.<break time="450ms"/>
The card was there.<break time="500ms"/>
"Miracle," she said.<break time="400ms"/>
"You set your expectations correctly."<break time="600ms"/>
A couple students laughed.<break time="450ms"/>
Ms. Alvarez did not.<break time="600ms"/>
That was unusual.<break time="600ms"/>
Kade watched her.<break time="500ms"/>
"You nervous?"<break time="350ms"/>
"For you?"<break time="350ms"/>
"For the national integrity of standardized testing."<break time="450ms"/>
"No."<break time="350ms"/>
"Good."<break time="500ms"/>
"I am, however, concerned you'll decide the instructions are philosophically inconsistent and refuse to answer question twelve."<break time="500ms"/>
"That happened once."<break time="300ms"/>
"Twice."<break time="350ms"/>
"The second one really was inconsistent."<break time="650ms"/>
Her mouth twitched.<break time="500ms"/>
Kade counted that as a win.<break time="700ms"/>
"Just take the test," she said.<break time="450ms"/>
He shrugged.<break time="1000ms"/>
||
The Aptitude Ascension Examination had been around longer than Kade had been alive. Every seventeen-year-old in the country took some version of it. The official explanation was that it measured academic reasoning, spatial cognition, reaction speed, systems intuition, stress adaptation, and a dozen other things useful for university admissions, technical apprenticeships, military programs, and Resonance screening.<break time="700ms"/>
That last category was why people cared.<break time="800ms"/>
Thirty-one years after Resonance entered the world, civilization had reorganized itself around the tiny percentage of human beings who could do things everyone else could only watch.<break time="800ms"/>
They stopped collapsing bridges.<break time="450ms"/>
Redirected aircraft during emergencies.<break time="450ms"/>
Worked orbital construction.<break time="450ms"/>
Stabilized coastal barriers during storms.<break time="450ms"/>
Competed in arenas watched by hundreds of millions.<break time="450ms"/>
Wore Conduits worth more than Northline's entire building.<break time="800ms"/>
And every year, a few teenagers walked into testing centers as ordinary kids and walked out with numbers that changed their lives.<break time="800ms"/>
Kade did not expect to be one of them.<break time="700ms"/>
He had learned to distrust any institution promising to reveal what he was capable of.<break time="600ms"/>
Institutions had been explaining what he was capable of for years.<break time="600ms"/>
Mostly they seemed disappointed.<break time="1000ms"/>
A soft chime sounded from the teacher's station.<break time="550ms"/>
Ms. Alvarez checked the message and looked around Room Four.<break time="600ms"/>
"All right. Ten-minute break before testing. Stay on campus. Nobody set anything on fire."<break time="600ms"/>
Darius raised a hand.<break time="400ms"/>
"Clarification."<break time="300ms"/>
"No."<break time="350ms"/>
"You didn't hear the question."<break time="350ms"/>
"I know you."<break time="700ms"/>
Chairs scraped back.<break time="550ms"/>
Kade stayed seated long enough to close his tablet.<break time="600ms"/>
A girl named Tessa passed his desk and tapped his shoulder.<break time="500ms"/>
"What do you think you'll score?"<break time="400ms"/>
"Between zero and whatever the maximum is."<break time="450ms"/>
"That confident?"<break time="350ms"/>
"I like to leave room for outcomes."<break time="500ms"/>
"My cousin got seven twelve last year."<break time="400ms"/>
"Is that good?"<break time="450ms"/>
She stared at him.<break time="400ms"/>
"You know it is."<break time="500ms"/>
He did.<break time="400ms"/>
Everyone did.<break time="700ms"/>
Scores above seven hundred triggered scholarship calls. Eight hundred brought recruiters. Nine hundred became local news.<break time="700ms"/>
The highest score ever recorded was supposedly nine sixty-eight, though there were arguments online about whether the scoring system had changed since then.<break time="600ms"/>
Northline students did not generally contribute to that debate.<break time="800ms"/>
Tessa leaned closer.<break time="450ms"/>
"My aunt says anything over six fifty and they test you for Conduit compatibility."<break time="500ms"/>
"Your aunt also says the moon landing was filmed in Nevada."<break time="500ms"/>
"That was my other aunt."<break time="400ms"/>
"You have a complicated family."<break time="600ms"/>
She gave him a finger and walked away.<break time="800ms"/>
Darius waited until she was gone.<break time="500ms"/>
"You ever think about it?"<break time="350ms"/>
"What?"<break time="350ms"/>
"Getting picked."<break time="600ms"/>
Kade knew exactly what he meant.<break time="500ms"/>
Everyone did.<break time="600ms"/>
He looked at the scratched plastic edge of his desk.<break time="600ms"/>
"No."<break time="500ms"/>
Darius frowned.<break time="450ms"/>
"That was too fast."<break time="350ms"/>
"I've had seventeen years."<break time="400ms"/>
"Nobody thinks about being Resonant at three."<break time="400ms"/>
"I was advanced."<break time="400ms"/>
"I'm serious."<break time="550ms"/>
Kade looked at him.<break time="450ms"/>
Darius was serious too.<break time="550ms"/>
That happened less often than his school file suggested.<break time="700ms"/>
"Sure," Kade said. "When I was twelve."<break time="400ms"/>
"And?"<break time="350ms"/>
"And then I turned thirteen."<break time="400ms"/>
"That doesn't mean anything."<break time="350ms"/>
"It means I got older."<break time="600ms"/>
Darius rolled his eyes.<break time="450ms"/>
Kade smiled.<break time="600ms"/>
The smile did what he wanted it to do.<break time="500ms"/>
Conversation over.<break time="800ms"/>
That was one of the useful things about jokes. People thought they were openings.<break time="600ms"/>
Mostly they were doors he could close without anybody noticing.<break time="1200ms"/>
||
At ten fifty-six, Ms. Alvarez stopped him at the classroom door.<break time="600ms"/>
The others filed past.<break time="600ms"/>
"Kade."<break time="450ms"/>
He turned.<break time="500ms"/>
She leaned against the teacher's desk with her arms folded.<break time="550ms"/>
"What?"<break time="350ms"/>
"Nothing."<break time="450ms"/>
"That's usually not why people say my name like that."<break time="600ms"/>
She studied him.<break time="600ms"/>
"You know what I think your problem is?"<break time="450ms"/>
"Alphabetical or ranked?"<break time="650ms"/>
"You decide something doesn't matter before you find out whether you're good at it."<break time="800ms"/>
His smile stayed in place.<break time="500ms"/>
"Efficient."<break time="500ms"/>
"No."<break time="700ms"/>
The word landed harder than it should have.<break time="800ms"/>
Ms. Alvarez nodded toward the hallway.<break time="500ms"/>
"Go take the test."<break time="600ms"/>
He turned away.<break time="550ms"/>
"And Kade?"<break time="500ms"/>
He looked back.<break time="600ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">"Try."</prosody><break time="900ms"/>
He almost made a joke.<break time="600ms"/>
For some reason, he didn't.<break time="1600ms"/>
Multipurpose Two had once been the county archive room.<break time="700ms"/>
The shelves were gone, replaced by twenty testing stations separated by collapsible privacy panels. Eleven Northline students sat in assigned seats while two district proctors checked identification cards.<break time="700ms"/>
Kade took Station Seven.<break time="700ms"/>
The testing tablet was larger than the ones used in class. A black sensor band rested beside it, thin enough to look harmless and expensive enough that Kade resisted the urge to ask what would happen if he dropped it.<break time="700ms"/>
A proctor stopped beside him.<break time="550ms"/>
"Temple contacts here and here," the man said, tapping the band. "Rear contacts behind the ears. Do not remove it during a section unless instructed."<break time="500ms"/>
"I've used one."<break time="400ms"/>
"Then this should go beautifully."<break time="600ms"/>
Kade looked at him.<break time="450ms"/>
The proctor smiled.<break time="500ms"/>
Kade decided he liked him.<break time="700ms"/>
He fitted the band around his head.<break time="500ms"/>
Cool contacts settled against his temples.<break time="550ms"/>
The screen illuminated.<break time="700ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">National Aptitude Ascension Examination.<break time="350ms"/> Candidate:<break time="200ms"/> Mercer, Kade A.<break time="300ms"/> Age: seventeen.<break time="300ms"/> Identity verified.</prosody><break time="800ms"/>
Below it, a block of legal text appeared.<break time="550ms"/>
Kade scrolled.<break time="400ms"/>
And scrolled.<break time="400ms"/>
And scrolled.<break time="600ms"/>
"Does anybody read this?" he asked.<break time="500ms"/>
The proctor did not look up.<break time="450ms"/>
"No."<break time="400ms"/>
"Comforting."<break time="400ms"/>
"Government efficiency."<break time="700ms"/>
Kade pressed accept.<break time="700ms"/>
A countdown appeared.<break time="600ms"/>
Five.<break time="500ms"/>
Four.<break time="500ms"/>
Three.<break time="550ms"/>
He rubbed his thumb against the edge of the desk.<break time="500ms"/>
Two.<break time="550ms"/>
Ms. Alvarez's voice returned unexpectedly in his head.<break time="500ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Try.</prosody><break time="600ms"/>
One.<break time="1100ms"/>
||
The first section was ordinary.<break time="500ms"/>
Pattern recognition.<break time="350ms"/>
Language.<break time="350ms"/>
Mathematics.<break time="550ms"/>
Kade moved quickly.<break time="500ms"/>
He had always been good at tests when he cared enough to finish them.<break time="600ms"/>
Sentence relationships. Rotating shapes. Conditional logic. Probability.<break time="550ms"/>
A few questions were difficult enough to be interesting.<break time="450ms"/>
Most were not.<break time="700ms"/>
Section four switched to dynamic systems.<break time="600ms"/>
A simulated suspension bridge oscillated in crosswind. Kade had to choose where to add damping.<break time="450ms"/>
Easy.<break time="500ms"/>
A municipal water network ruptured. He had to isolate the failure while preserving pressure to a hospital district.<break time="450ms"/>
Better.<break time="500ms"/>
A transport grid lost three substations during an evacuation. He had ninety seconds to reroute power and traffic simultaneously.<break time="500ms"/>
He forgot to be bored.<break time="550ms"/>
That annoyed him a little.<break time="700ms"/>
The questions stopped feeling like school.<break time="600ms"/>
For once, the test seemed to be asking what he actually saw when he looked at a problem.<break time="500ms"/>
Not whether he remembered an equation.<break time="600ms"/>
What failed first?<break time="400ms"/>
What could be sacrificed?<break time="400ms"/>
What looked important but wasn't?<break time="400ms"/>
Where was the load really going?<break time="700ms"/>
He leaned closer to the screen.<break time="600ms"/>
In one simulation, three emergency routes converged at a bridge rated below projected capacity.<break time="500ms"/>
The obvious answer was to reroute traffic.<break time="500ms"/>
Kade left the traffic alone and shut down two commercial substations instead, darkening several blocks and freeing enough grid capacity to accelerate a rail evacuation line.<break time="600ms"/>
The simulation paused.<break time="500ms"/>
A message appeared.<break time="600ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Civilian impact penalty.<break time="250ms"/> Minus eight point three percent.</prosody><break time="600ms"/>
Then:<break time="450ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Survival projection.<break time="250ms"/> Plus fourteen point seven percent.</prosody><break time="700ms"/>
Kade stared at it.<break time="600ms"/>
"Rude," he whispered.<break time="600ms"/>
He accepted the score.<break time="900ms"/>
At some point, the first students began finishing.<break time="500ms"/>
Kade noticed because a chair scraped behind him.<break time="450ms"/>
Then another.<break time="500ms"/>
He kept going.<break time="500ms"/>
The room thinned.<break time="700ms"/>
Section five was reaction timing.<break time="400ms"/>
Section six was adaptive reasoning.<break time="500ms"/>
Kade expected the system to become harder.<break time="500ms"/>
Instead it became stranger.<break time="700ms"/>
Questions stopped presenting all the information at once.<break time="500ms"/>
A collision simulation changed mass after he committed to an answer.<break time="400ms"/>
A pressure network hid two sensors.<break time="400ms"/>
A structural problem gave him three plausible assumptions and penalized him for choosing any of them too early.<break time="550ms"/>
That last one made him smile.<break time="550ms"/>
Finally.<break time="450ms"/>
A test that admitted the problem might be the problem.<break time="1000ms"/>
||
Then Section Seven loaded.<break time="800ms"/>
There was no title.<break time="600ms"/>
Only one sentence.<break time="700ms"/>
<prosody rate="86%">Identify the boundary of the active system.</prosody><break time="900ms"/>
A diagram appeared.<break time="600ms"/>
A steel sphere hung from a cable inside a transparent chamber. The chamber sat on a platform. The platform rested on a larger floor grid. Colored arrows indicated force vectors.<break time="650ms"/>
Kade frowned.<break time="550ms"/>
There was no question beyond the instruction.<break time="550ms"/>
He waited.<break time="500ms"/>
Nothing happened.<break time="600ms"/>
He tapped the sphere.<break time="450ms"/>
The tablet chimed.<break time="450ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Incomplete.</prosody><break time="500ms"/>
He tapped the chamber.<break time="350ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Incomplete.</prosody><break time="450ms"/>
The platform.<break time="300ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Incomplete.</prosody><break time="700ms"/>
Kade leaned back.<break time="600ms"/>
The sensor contacts at his temples felt colder now.<break time="700ms"/>
He looked at the diagram again.<break time="600ms"/>
The drawing wanted him to pretend the lines around the objects mattered.<break time="600ms"/>
Sphere.<break time="300ms"/>
Cable.<break time="300ms"/>
Chamber.<break time="300ms"/>
Platform.<break time="300ms"/>
Floor.<break time="550ms"/>
Nice clean boxes.<break time="700ms"/>
But the cable attached to the chamber ceiling. The chamber transferred load to the platform. The platform transferred force into the floor. The floor into the foundation. The foundation into the earth.<break time="600ms"/>
And if the sphere moved, energy went into all of it.<break time="800ms"/>
"That's not a system," Kade whispered.<break time="650ms"/>
The proctor glanced over.<break time="500ms"/>
Kade ignored him.<break time="700ms"/>
He dragged his finger around the sphere, cable, chamber and platform.<break time="450ms"/>
The selection glowed.<break time="400ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Incomplete.</prosody><break time="550ms"/>
He expanded it to include the floor grid.<break time="500ms"/>
The screen flickered.<break time="500ms"/>
Just once.<break time="700ms"/>
Kade stopped.<break time="550ms"/>
He removed his finger.<break time="450ms"/>
The diagram returned to normal.<break time="500ms"/>
He drew the same boundary again.<break time="500ms"/>
Another flicker.<break time="550ms"/>
Not random.<break time="700ms"/>
Kade's pulse changed.<break time="700ms"/>
He expanded the selection beyond the floor grid.<break time="400ms"/>
The interface refused.<break time="400ms"/>
He tried again.<break time="400ms"/>
Nothing.<break time="600ms"/>
Then he noticed a tiny change at the edge of the diagram.<break time="550ms"/>
The selection handle had moved.<break time="500ms"/>
Before, it snapped to objects.<break time="400ms"/>
Now it floated between them.<break time="700ms"/>
Kade touched it.<break time="550ms"/>
The boundary moved.<break time="500ms"/>
Anywhere.<break time="800ms"/>
He sat up.<break time="500ms"/>
The room seemed quieter.<break time="700ms"/>
He drew a line that cut diagonally through the platform.<break time="350ms"/>
The screen accepted it.<break time="400ms"/>
He drew upward, excluding one support and including the other.<break time="350ms"/>
Accepted.<break time="350ms"/>
Across the chamber wall.<break time="300ms"/>
Accepted.<break time="300ms"/>
Through the cable.<break time="300ms"/>
Accepted.<break time="400ms"/>
Around half the sphere.<break time="700ms"/>
The screen went black.<break time="900ms"/>
Kade froze.<break time="700ms"/>
For one full second, he saw only his own reflection.<break time="800ms"/>
Then a single word appeared in white.<break time="900ms"/>
<prosody rate="74%">Redefine.</prosody><break time="1100ms"/>
His heartbeat kicked.<break time="600ms"/>
"What?"<break time="700ms"/>
The word vanished.<break time="1000ms"/>
A new simulation loaded.<break time="600ms"/>
No section header.<break time="450ms"/>
No instructions beyond:<break time="550ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Minimize occupant impulse.</prosody><break time="700ms"/>
A vehicle struck a barrier.<break time="500ms"/>
Kade selected the crumple zones.<break time="400ms"/>
<prosody rate="90%">Eighty-three point one percent.</prosody><break time="500ms"/>
He reset it.<break time="350ms"/>
Included the barrier.<break time="350ms"/>
<prosody rate="90%">Ninety-one point four percent.</prosody><break time="450ms"/>
Again.<break time="300ms"/>
Included a section of roadway.<break time="350ms"/>
<prosody rate="90%">Ninety-six point zero percent.</prosody><break time="450ms"/>
Again.<break time="400ms"/>
This time he excluded the vehicle's rear axle from the active transfer network.<break time="500ms"/>
The screen hesitated.<break time="550ms"/>
For half a second, the numbers dissolved into static.<break time="650ms"/>
Then:<break time="500ms"/>
<prosody rate="86%">Ninety-nine point nine eight percent.</prosody><break time="900ms"/>
Kade stared.<break time="700ms"/>
A pressure began behind his eyes.<break time="500ms"/>
Not pain.<break time="400ms"/>
Not yet.<break time="600ms"/>
More like the sensation of remembering a word he had never learned.<break time="800ms"/>
The next problem loaded before he touched anything.<break time="550ms"/>
A pressure vessel.<break time="250ms"/>
Prevent rupture.<break time="300ms"/>
He changed the boundary.<break time="400ms"/>
A power grid.<break time="250ms"/>
Preserve critical load.<break time="250ms"/>
Boundary.<break time="350ms"/>
A falling structure.<break time="250ms"/>
Minimize transferred impulse.<break time="250ms"/>
Boundary.<break time="400ms"/>
Heat.<break time="180ms"/>
Charge.<break time="180ms"/>
Fluid flow.<break time="180ms"/>
Collisions.<break time="500ms"/>
The questions came faster.<break time="400ms"/>
At first Kade answered them.<break time="350ms"/>
Then he started anticipating them.<break time="450ms"/>
The interface seemed to anticipate him back.<break time="700ms"/>
Every problem asked the same hidden question in different clothes.<break time="600ms"/>
Where did the thing end?<break time="700ms"/>
And every time, Kade's answer became simpler.<break time="700ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">It ended wherever you stopped counting.</prosody><break time="1000ms"/>
||
The pressure behind his eyes sharpened.<break time="550ms"/>
He blinked hard.<break time="600ms"/>
A timer in the corner showed eighty-seven thirteen.<break time="650ms"/>
That could not be right.<break time="550ms"/>
The standard exam block was ninety minutes total.<break time="500ms"/>
He had already been testing longer than that.<break time="700ms"/>
He looked toward the proctor.<break time="600ms"/>
The man was standing very still.<break time="600ms"/>
Not bored-still.<break time="500ms"/>
Watching-still.<break time="800ms"/>
Kade looked back at the screen.<break time="550ms"/>
The timer disappeared.<break time="800ms"/>
A new problem appeared.<break time="550ms"/>
No diagram this time.<break time="500ms"/>
Only a blank gray field and one sentence.<break time="750ms"/>
<prosody rate="84%">Define the active system.</prosody><break time="1000ms"/>
Kade waited.<break time="600ms"/>
Nothing appeared.<break time="500ms"/>
No objects.<break time="350ms"/>
No vectors.<break time="350ms"/>
No boundaries.<break time="700ms"/>
"Seriously?" he whispered.<break time="700ms"/>
His first instinct was to tap the whole screen.<break time="550ms"/>
He stopped himself.<break time="800ms"/>
What active system?<break time="600ms"/>
The tablet?<break time="400ms"/>
The testing station?<break time="400ms"/>
The room?<break time="500ms"/>
Him?<break time="900ms"/>
He looked at his hand resting on the desk.<break time="450ms"/>
At the cable running from the tablet into the floor.<break time="400ms"/>
At the privacy panels.<break time="400ms"/>
At the proctor.<break time="450ms"/>
At the sensor band touching his skin.<break time="800ms"/>
Something cold moved down his back.<break time="900ms"/>
Kade placed two fingers on the blank screen and drew a boundary around nothing.<break time="900ms"/>
The tablet vibrated once.<break time="600ms"/>
The gray field vanished.<break time="700ms"/>
<prosody rate="86%">Section complete.</prosody><break time="1100ms"/>
He sat there for several seconds.<break time="700ms"/>
Then the normal testing interface returned as if nothing had happened.<break time="700ms"/>
At the top:<break time="500ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Scoring.</prosody><break time="900ms"/>
Kade looked around.<break time="600ms"/>
Multipurpose Two was almost empty.<break time="600ms"/>
One student remained at the far end, packing her bag.<break time="500ms"/>
A proctor stood by the door.<break time="450ms"/>
The other stood behind Kade's station, three meters away.<break time="700ms"/>
"How long was I in that section?" Kade asked.<break time="600ms"/>
The closer proctor looked at the wall clock.<break time="500ms"/>
Then at him.<break time="600ms"/>
<prosody rate="90%">"Long enough."</prosody><break time="700ms"/>
"That sounds official."<break time="600ms"/>
The man did not smile.<break time="900ms"/>
Kade removed the sensor band.<break time="550ms"/>
His skin felt cool where the contacts had rested.<break time="500ms"/>
A drop of sweat moved from his hairline toward his temple.<break time="900ms"/>
||
The door opened.<break time="600ms"/>
Darius leaned his head inside.<break time="500ms"/>
"You alive?"<break time="350ms"/>
"Debatable."<break time="400ms"/>
"Food's gone."<break time="350ms"/>
"Tragedy."<break time="450ms"/>
"Alvarez said I had to come get you because apparently abandoning people in government testing facilities is frowned upon."<break time="450ms"/>
"Authoritarian."<break time="700ms"/>
Darius stepped into the room.<break time="500ms"/>
The proctor opened his mouth.<break time="450ms"/>
Darius pointed behind himself.<break time="400ms"/>
"She said."<break time="600ms"/>
The proctor apparently knew Ms. Alvarez well enough to accept that as documentation.<break time="800ms"/>
Darius stopped beside Kade.<break time="500ms"/>
"What'd you get?"<break time="350ms"/>
"It's scoring."<break time="400ms"/>
"You think you're Resonant?"<break time="350ms"/>
"No."<break time="400ms"/>
"That sounded fast."<break time="350ms"/>
"Because I've had seventeen years to think about it."<break time="500ms"/>
"You'd be annoying as hell with powers."<break time="400ms"/>
"That's fair."<break time="400ms"/>
"You'd stop bullets and explain why everybody was standing wrong."<break time="400ms"/>
"I'd probably start with your posture."<break time="600ms"/>
Darius laughed.<break time="900ms"/>
The scoring icon vanished.<break time="700ms"/>
Kade turned back to the screen.<break time="700ms"/>
For a moment he thought it had failed to load.<break time="700ms"/>
The page was almost empty.<break time="600ms"/>
No percentile.<break time="400ms"/>
No section breakdown.<break time="400ms"/>
No recommendations.<break time="800ms"/>
Then three digits appeared in the center.<break time="1000ms"/>
<prosody rate="69%">Zero.<break time="320ms"/> Zero.<break time="320ms"/> Zero.</prosody><break time="1300ms"/>
Darius went silent.<break time="800ms"/>
Kade stared at the number.<break time="700ms"/>
The proctor behind him inhaled sharply.<break time="700ms"/>
That was interesting.<break time="900ms"/>
Darius recovered first.<break time="600ms"/>
"Wow."<break time="600ms"/>
Kade nodded slowly.<break time="600ms"/>
"Honestly, kind of impressive."<break time="500ms"/>
"You got zero."<break time="400ms"/>
"I noticed."<break time="400ms"/>
"Like zero zero."<break time="450ms"/>
"There are three of them. It's a strong commitment."<break time="650ms"/>
Darius barked a laugh.<break time="500ms"/>
Kade smiled too.<break time="800ms"/>
Because that was what you did.<break time="600ms"/>
You made the joke before somebody else could.<break time="600ms"/>
You made failure funny enough that nobody had to ask whether it hurt.<break time="1000ms"/>
Three schools.<break time="400ms"/>
Three suspensions.<break time="400ms"/>
A semester he could have passed.<break time="400ms"/>
Forty-three days at Northline.<break time="700ms"/>
And now the grand national machine designed to identify hidden potential had looked at Kade Mercer and returned the cleanest possible answer.<break time="900ms"/>
<prosody rate="82%">Nothing.</prosody><break time="1200ms"/>
||
He waited for the embarrassment.<break time="600ms"/>
It came.<break time="500ms"/>
Not hard.<break time="400ms"/>
Just familiar.<break time="600ms"/>
A little pressure beneath the ribs.<break time="450ms"/>
A little heat at the back of the neck.<break time="800ms"/>
The old instinct arrived with it.<break time="600ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Doesn't matter.</prosody><break time="800ms"/>
He could already hear himself telling Ms. Alvarez.<break time="600ms"/>
Stupid test.<break time="350ms"/>
Bad scoring.<break time="350ms"/>
Government software.<break time="700ms"/>
He could make the whole thing irrelevant before he ever had to decide whether he had cared.<break time="900ms"/>
He looked at the three zeros.<break time="900ms"/>
Ms. Alvarez's voice came back again.<break time="700ms"/>
<prosody rate="86%">You decide something doesn't matter before you find out whether you're good at it.</prosody><break time="1000ms"/>
Kade's smile faded.<break time="800ms"/>
For once, he had tried.<break time="700ms"/>
That was the annoying part.<break time="700ms"/>
He had actually tried.<break time="1100ms"/>
Behind him, the proctor said, very quietly, <prosody rate="86%">"Don't touch anything."</prosody><break time="900ms"/>
Kade turned.<break time="500ms"/>
"What?"<break time="800ms"/>
The lights went out.<break time="900ms"/>
Not dimmed.<break time="500ms"/>
Out.<break time="800ms"/>
The ventilation stopped.<break time="550ms"/>
Every tablet in Multipurpose Two turned black at the same instant.<break time="600ms"/>
Darius's laugh died.<break time="800ms"/>
Emergency lights should have come on.<break time="600ms"/>
They didn't.<break time="800ms"/>
For two seconds, the room was completely dark.<break time="800ms"/>
Kade heard somebody inhale.<break time="800ms"/>
Then every testing screen illuminated white.<break time="800ms"/>
No logos.<break time="350ms"/>
No controls.<break time="500ms"/>
Just a single line of black text.<break time="800ms"/>
