Outside Multipurpose Two, doors opened up and down the hallway. Teachers called students back into rooms. Phones chimed. Somewhere, Russell was arguing loudly with someone about district emergency procedures.<break time="900ms"/>
Inside, Station Seven continued glowing.<break time="900ms"/>
<prosody rate="70%">Zero.<break time="300ms"/> Zero.<break time="300ms"/> Zero.</prosody><break time="1200ms"/>
<prosody rate="88%">Kade had spent most of his life being told that numbers measured things.</prosody><break time="900ms"/>
Grades.<break time="400ms"/>
Attendance.<break time="400ms"/>
Suspensions.<break time="400ms"/>
Risk.<break time="400ms"/>
Potential.<break time="400ms"/>
Success.<break time="400ms"/>
Failure.<break time="1000ms"/>
<prosody rate="86%">This was the first number he had ever received that seemed to frighten the people who had given it to him.</prosody><break time="1200ms"/>
<prosody rate="82%">And for the first time that day, Kade wondered if zero meant something other than nothing.</prosody><break time="1500ms"/>
